[Do's and Don'ts for an Ascender]

> Do:
> Read every spoiler you can get your hands on (it makes the game 100
> time more fun for me).
This has to be your own choice: only get spoiled when you really,
REALLY _REALLY_ want to ascend. Remember that, once you are spoiled,
you can't be un-spoiled. This sounds like a stupid remark, and perhaps
it is a stupid remark, but I found the game only half as fun once I'm
spoiled. You know al the answers to the questions the game poses you,
and there is about no need to be creative. Well, you have to be
creative a lot of times, but make sure you're not going to regret your
choice.
Having this said, I just know you're going to read the spoilers anyway
- everyone does eventually

> Post questions to this newsgroup, no matter how dumb you think they
> are (look at some of my posts for example).
Indeed. Don't hesitate to ask questions, repeat questions if needed,
just don't annoy people. And don't ask too many questions in one post,
someone will reply to one question and the whole discussion will go
towards that one question...
And don't forget the power of 'Google Groups', but I have the idea you
already now this...

> Stick to playing the same character for at least a little while, so
> you get used to that characters quirks.
Yes and no: you don't want to be dependant of one role's/race's
abilities. It is mention in the Guidebook I think (or in the FAQ or
some other document). When you play a priest for example, you could
become used too much to it's ability of recognising the b/u/c status
of an item... thus having some inpleasant surprises next time.
OTOH, you wand to 'specialize' after a while of playing. Focusing on
valks, for instance, can help you getting your tactics optimised. Just
don't be surprised you won't ascend a wizard the next time.

> Get your buddies interested and have your own nethack tournaments with
> cash payouts to the winner (I did). Also a good idea to make sure they
> are worse than you.
Neat. Have to try that myself.

> Colaborate as much as possible to get as informed about the game as
> you can.
Once again, this is your own decision

> BE PATIENT!
Indeed. Also:
HAVE FUN!
when it is possible, at least. Don't get frustrated because you loose
a character for some stupid reason (you *don't* want to die on the way
back up by touching a cockatrice barehanded, like me), but laugh with
it and learn from your experience.

